            <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
            <div class="mt-16">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                Common Mistakes Choosing Between Oil-Based and Water-Based Sealers
              </h2>

              <p>
                The two families apply differently, cure differently, and suit different conditions. Choosing on price alone is how people end up disappointed with both.
              </p>

              <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Water-Based Sealer in Cool or Humid Weather</h3>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                    Water-based products cure by evaporation, which effectively stops in cool, humid, or still conditions. A coat that will not dry stays soft, streaks, and tracks. They need warmth and airflow far more than solvent-based products do.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Ventilation Demands of Solvent-Based Products</h3>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                    Oil and solvent-based sealers give off substantial vapour during application and cure. On an enclosed driveway or near open windows, that is a real practical constraint — and in some jurisdictions a regulatory one due to VOC limits.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Type Over the Other</h3>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                    The two chemistries do not reliably bond to one another, and a water-based coat over a sound solvent-based film can lift or fail in patches. If you are switching families, either strip back to sound pavement or expect the transition coat to underperform.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                  <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Water-Based Means Lower Performance</h3>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                    Modern water-based sealers have closed much of the durability gap and are considerably easier and safer to apply, with soap-and-water cleanup. On a residential driveway in a warm dry climate, the practical difference is smaller than the price difference suggests.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Closing -->
            <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
              <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
              <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                The <strong class="text-white">oil-based versus water-based</strong> decision usually comes down to your climate and your local rules rather than raw performance. Solvent-based sealers penetrate better and tolerate cool, damp application conditions; water-based ones are lower-odour, easier to clean up, and increasingly the only option where VOC limits apply.
              </p>
              <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                Whichever family you choose, do not mix them on the same surface, and match the application to the product: warmth and airflow for water-based, ventilation and care for solvent-based. Both perform well inside their conditions and poorly outside them.
              </p>
            </div>

